    "steps": [
      "The guest searches by city, dates and party size. The search service reads a denormalised availability and rate cache, finds hotels where every night in the range has stock, and labels results as indicative, not guaranteed.",
      "At checkout the booking service creates a pending reservation with an idempotency key and starts a saga: hold inventory, take payment, confirm.",
      "The inventory service holds the chosen room type for every night of the stay in one database transaction. It uses a conditional update (held + sold < limit) on each room_type-night row, writes a hold record with an expiry time, and schedules a release on the delay queue.",
      "The limit for each row is the physical room count plus the overbooking allowance set by revenue management for that room type and date. If any night fails the check, the whole hold rolls back and the guest sees alternatives.",
      "The booking service authorises the card or collects the deposit set by the rate plan's policy. It confirms the booking only after the gateway webhook succeeds, and converts the hold into sold in the same transaction as the status change.",
      "Every inventory change writes an outbox row in the same transaction. A relay publishes these rows to Kafka, where consumers refresh the search cache and send notifications.",
      "The channel manager turns events into availability, rate and inventory (ARI) deltas for each OTA and pushes them. It also takes in OTA reservations and modifications and sends them through the same booking and inventory path, using the OTA's booking id as the idempotency key.",
      "Cancellations apply the rate plan's policy, such as free-cancel window, penalty or non-refundable. The service releases each night back to inventory, starts any refund, and emits events so OTAs and search show the room again."
    ]
