    "steps": [
      "The client opens a WebSocket to the gateway, which authenticates the user and asks the ACL service for their role (owner, editor, commenter, viewer) on that document; viewers get a read-only session.",
      "The gateway routes the connection to the single session server that owns the document (via consistent hashing or a lease). That server loads the latest snapshot plus the operation-log tail and sends the client the document at revision N.",
      "When the user types, the client applies the change locally at once and sends an operation tagged with its base revision (OT) or with unique element IDs and a vector clock (CRDT).",
      "The session server transforms the op against any concurrent ops since its base revision (OT) or merges it (CRDT), assigns the next revision, and durably appends it to the operation log before acknowledging the sender.",
      "The accepted op is published on the document's fan-out channel so every gateway holding a connection for that document pushes it to its clients, which transform or merge it into their local state.",
      "Cursor positions, selections and who-is-here are sent as throttled, ephemeral presence messages with a short TTL; they are broadcast but never written to the operation log.",
      "If the connection drops, the client keeps editing and stores ops in IndexedDB. On reconnect it fetches missed ops since its last acknowledged revision and replays its queue, which is rebased (OT) or merged (CRDT) without user action.",
      "Comments are stored separately and anchored to stable positions (CRDT element IDs or ranges transformed alongside edits), so they move with text; the comment and suggest permissions are enforced server-side on every op.",
      "A background compactor writes a snapshot every N ops so loads stay fast; version history serves any revision by loading the nearest earlier snapshot and replaying ops, and a change feed drives notifications and search indexing."
    ]
