    "steps": [
      "The customer sends a question in the bank's web or app chat, for example asking about debit card replacement charges or how to update a nominee.",
      "The chat API authenticates the session and resolves a server-side thread_id for this conversation and a user_id for the customer. It invokes the agent with config {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": ...}} and passes user_id as runtime context, never taking either value from the message text.",
      "PIIMiddleware runs before the model and masks or redacts patterns such as phone numbers, emails and card or account numbers. Raw identifiers therefore never reach the model, the checkpoint or the summary.",
      "The agent, built with create_agent and a Postgres checkpointer, loads the thread's saved message state, so follow-ups like \"and for a credit card?\" resolve against earlier turns.",
      "A custom before_model middleware reads the customer's preferences from the LangGraph store under a namespace such as (\"users\", user_id, \"preferences\"). It adds them to the system prompt, for example preferred language and contact channel.",
      "When the conversation crosses the SummarizationMiddleware trigger (for example (\"tokens\", 4000)), older messages are replaced by a model-written summary. The most recent messages are kept verbatim (for example (\"messages\", 20)).",
      "The model answers from the bank's approved FAQ content by calling a search_faq retriever tool over a vector store. It cites the FAQ entry instead of answering from its own general knowledge.",
      "If the customer states a lasting preference, the model calls save_preference. The tool validates the field against an allow-list and writes it to the store through the runtime store, scoped to the runtime user_id.",
      "Questions that need account data or a transaction action, such as disputes or balances, are not answered by the FAQ bot. The agent escalates to a human agent and passes along the thread summary, while the reply streams back and events are written to the audit log."
    ]
