    "steps": [
      "The analyst sends a goal to the Agent Server with a thread_id. The graph is compiled with a Postgres checkpointer, and the state is a TypedDict with input, plan (list[str]), past_steps (Annotated[list, operator.add]) and response.",
      "The planner node pulls similar past plans from the PostgresStore namespace (team, 'plans') using semantic search. It calls the model with structured output and returns {'plan': [...]}, a short ordered list of self-contained steps.",
      "The plan review node calls interrupt() with the draft plan. The client resumes with Command(resume=...): an approval continues to the executor, and an edit returns Command(goto='planner', update=...) carrying the feedback.",
      "The executor takes plan[0] and runs it through a tool-calling agent subgraph with a scoped tool set (read-only SQL, web search, MCP tools). It returns {'past_steps': [(step, result)]}, and the operator.add reducer appends this to the history.",
      "The replanner sees the goal, the current plan and all past_steps. It returns either a new list of only the remaining steps, which overwrites plan, or a final Response, using one structured-output schema with a union action.",
      "The should_end conditional edge routes to END when response is set, and back to the executor otherwise. A step budget in state and the run's recursion_limit stop runaway loops and force a best-effort answer.",
      "The checkpointer saves state at every super-step. A crashed run resumes from its last checkpoint. Engineers use get_state_history to inspect runs and update_state to fork from an earlier step with a corrected plan.",
      "The client streams with stream_mode=['updates','messages']. Updates show plan changes and completed steps, and messages carry LLM tokens tagged with node metadata, so the UI can label which node is speaking.",
      "On END, a summary of the plan and outcome is written to the PostgresStore for future planning. LangSmith traces are used to evaluate plan quality, replan counts and final answers."
    ]
