    "steps": [
      "The engineer opens the failing conversation in LangSmith, notes its thread_id and points a debug copy of the graph at the same checkpointer. In that copy, side-effecting tools such as refunds and emails are swapped for sandboxed versions.",
      "Call graph.get_state_history({\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": tid}}) to list StateSnapshots, newest first. For each snapshot, inspect values, next, tasks and metadata (step, source, writes) to see what every super-step changed.",
      "Pick the snapshot whose next is ('tools',), where the last AIMessage holds the tool call that returned bad data. Keep its config, which carries the checkpoint_id.",
      "Replay by calling graph.invoke(None, snapshot.config). Steps before the checkpoint are not re-executed; nodes after it run again, which shows whether the failure reproduces or was caused by model nondeterminism.",
      "Fork by calling graph.update_state(snapshot.config, {\"messages\": [ToolMessage(content=corrected, tool_call_id=call_id)]}, as_node=\"tools\"). This writes a new checkpoint on a branch and leaves the original history untouched.",
      "Resume the fork with graph.stream(None, fork_config, stream_mode=\"updates\"). The graph continues from the edges after the tools node, so the agent reasons over the substituted tool result.",
      "Compare the final states of the original run, the replay and the fork: the messages, the tool calls chosen and the final reply. If the fork behaves correctly, the tool result was the cause; if it still fails, the prompt or model logic is at fault.",
      "Apply the fix, which may be tool output validation, a schema change or a prompt change. Add the checkpointed input as a LangSmith dataset example and re-run it to confirm the fix before shipping."
    ]
