    "steps": [
      "The applicant submits details and documents. The API starts a graph run on the parent StateGraph with thread_id set to the application ID, so every checkpoint and resume belongs to that one application.",
      "The intake node validates the input and writes the documents to encrypted object storage. It keeps only document references and minimal fields in state. Three edges from START-side intake fan out to the identity, address and sanctions wrapper nodes, which run in the same super-step.",
      "The identity subgraph has its own typed state for OCR output, face-match score, liveness result and retry count. It calls vendor APIs, retries transient failures inside the subgraph, and returns only a compact result object (status, reason codes, evidence refs) to the parent.",
      "The address subgraph matches proof-of-address data against the declared address. The sanctions subgraph screens the name and date of birth against sanctions and PEP lists and returns any potential hits with match details. Neither subgraph exposes its internal scratch state to the parent.",
      "The merge node is wired with add_edge from all three wrapper nodes, so it runs only after every branch finishes. The parent state key 'checks' uses a reducer (e.g. operator.add on a list), so parallel writes accumulate instead of conflicting. The Postgres checkpointer saves state at every super-step.",
      "A conditional edge on the merged checks routes the case. If all three are 'pass', it goes straight to account opening. Any 'fail', 'inconclusive', vendor error or sanctions hit goes to manual review, so the default path for anything unexpected is review.",
      "The review node first has an LLM draft a short case brief from the structured evidence only; the LLM summarises and never decides. The node then calls interrupt() with the brief. The run pauses durably until the analyst's decision arrives through Command(resume=...).",
      "The decision is applied: approve opens the account, reject closes the application with reason codes, and 'request more' uses Command(goto=..., update=...) to re-run only the affected subgraph. get_state_history on the thread gives the audit trail of every step."
    ]
